# Theme shortcuts: subtheme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`theme_sub_axis()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/subtheme.html), which covers
`theme_sub_axis_x()`, `theme_sub_axis_y()`, `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`, `theme_sub_axis_top()`,
`theme_sub_axis_left()`, `theme_sub_axis_right()`, `theme_sub_legend()`, `theme_sub_panel()`,
`theme_sub_plot()` and `theme_sub_strip()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example
shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2
code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These functions are shortcuts for `theme()` with shorter argument names: each sets the elements
of one part of the plot, so `theme_sub_axis_bottom(title = ...)` sets what
`theme(axis.title.x.bottom = ...)` sets. There is one for all the axes, for both x axes, for both
y axes and for each of the four, and one each for the legend, the panels, the whole plot and the
facet strips. Giggly draws every plot in ggplot2's default theme, `theme_gray()`, and has no way
to change the theme yet: neither these functions nor `theme()`.

## Styling the bottom axis with `theme()`

```r
# A standard plot
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(disp, mpg, colour = drat)) +
  geom_point()

red_text <- element_text(colour = "red")
red_line <- element_line(colour = "red")

# The theme settings below:
p + theme(
  axis.title.x.bottom = red_text,
  axis.text.x.bottom  = red_text,
  axis.line.x.bottom  = red_line,
  axis.ticks.x.bottom = red_line
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it and the legend that reads it back,
and changing the theme, here to draw the bottom axis's title, labels, line and ticks in red.

## The same with `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`

```r
# Are equivalent to these less verbose theme settings
p + theme_sub_axis_bottom(
  title = red_text,
  text  = red_text,
  line  = red_line,
  ticks = red_line
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it and the legend that reads it back,
and `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`.
